1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, the podcast where

2
00:00:03,488 --> 00:00:06,083
each week we explore the Torah portion through

3
00:00:06,103 --> 00:00:09,603
the eyes of Rashi, the great medieval commentator

4
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:12,723
whose insights have shaped Jewish learning for nearly

5
00:00:12,803 --> 00:00:14,083
a thousand years.

6
00:00:14,355 --> 00:00:18,963
This week we're looking at Parshat Lech-Lecha, which

7
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:22,883
covers Genesis chapter twelve through chapter seventeen, and

8
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:27,043
it's a portion absolutely brimming with drama, promise,

9
00:00:27,103 --> 00:00:28,883
and transformation.

10
00:00:29,023 --> 00:00:32,083
Let's dive in and see what Rashi notices

11
00:00:32,147 --> 00:00:37,135
that we might otherwise miss.Lech-Lecha is the portion

12
00:00:37,162 --> 00:00:39,855
where we meet Avram — later renamed Avraham

13
00:00:40,055 --> 00:00:42,655
— the first patriarch of the Jewish people.

14
00:00:42,795 --> 00:00:45,295
God tells him to leave his homeland and

15
00:00:45,352 --> 00:00:47,055
travel to a land that God will show

16
00:00:47,115 --> 00:00:49,535
him, promising to make him into a great

17
00:00:49,615 --> 00:00:50,415
nation.

18
00:00:50,535 --> 00:00:53,535
Along the way, Avram and his wife Sarai

19
00:00:53,605 --> 00:00:56,575
journey to Egypt during a famine, where Pharaoh

20
00:00:56,628 --> 00:00:59,455
takes Sarai into his palace, and God strikes

21
00:00:59,535 --> 00:01:01,615
Pharaoh with plagues to protect her.

22
00:01:01,802 --> 00:01:04,575
We also read about Avram's separation from his

23
00:01:04,621 --> 00:01:07,375
nephew Lot, a war between kings in which

24
00:01:07,482 --> 00:01:10,895
Avram rescues Lot, a dramatic covenant ceremony with

25
00:01:10,995 --> 00:01:14,175
God, the birth of Yishmael through Hagar, and

26
00:01:14,235 --> 00:01:17,535
finally God's command of circumcision and the renaming

27
00:01:17,562 --> 00:01:20,655
of both Avram and Sarai to Avraham and

28
00:01:20,722 --> 00:01:21,375
Sarah.

29
00:01:21,855 --> 00:01:25,455
Rashi, as always, is drawn to the small

30
00:01:25,545 --> 00:01:29,775
details — an unusual word, a grammatical oddity,

31
00:01:29,815 --> 00:01:32,255
a phrase that seems redundant — and he

32
00:01:32,335 --> 00:01:35,615
uses these tiny textual clues to unlock layers

33
00:01:35,668 --> 00:01:40,526
of meaning that enrich the entire narrative.Let's start

34
00:01:40,558 --> 00:01:42,926
with the very opening of the portion.

35
00:01:43,126 --> 00:01:46,846
God says to Avram, "Lech lecha," which literally

36
00:01:46,926 --> 00:01:50,366
means "Go for yourself," from your land, from

37
00:01:50,382 --> 00:01:53,526
your birthplace, and from your father's house.

38
00:01:53,676 --> 00:01:56,126
Now, most of us would describe a journey

39
00:01:56,206 --> 00:01:59,486
by starting small and going big — you'd

40
00:01:59,566 --> 00:02:02,846
say, leave your house, leave your city, leave

41
00:02:02,878 --> 00:02:03,966
your country.

42
00:02:04,166 --> 00:02:06,207
But the Torah does the opposite.

43
00:02:06,420 --> 00:02:10,127
It starts with "your land," the largest circle,

44
00:02:10,247 --> 00:02:13,567
and narrows down to "your father's house," the

45
00:02:13,647 --> 00:02:14,687
most intimate.

46
00:02:15,060 --> 00:02:18,447
Rashi picks up on this unusual order and

47
00:02:18,509 --> 00:02:21,167
explains that God structured the command this way

48
00:02:21,247 --> 00:02:25,247
to make each stage progressively more emotionally difficult.

49
00:02:25,387 --> 00:02:28,607
Leaving your country is hard, but leaving the

50
00:02:28,678 --> 00:02:31,807
specific place where you were born is harder,

51
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:34,447
and leaving your father's household — the people

52
00:02:34,507 --> 00:02:37,647
you love most — is hardest of all.

53
00:02:37,807 --> 00:02:41,647
God is acknowledging Avram's emotional reality, and that

54
00:02:41,714 --> 00:02:45,007
small observation by Rashi transforms what could feel

55
00:02:45,055 --> 00:02:48,367
like a simple travel itinerary into a portrait

56
00:02:48,394 --> 00:02:52,745
of profound sacrifice.Next, let's look at what happens

57
00:02:52,777 --> 00:02:55,465
when Avram and Sarai go down to Egypt.

58
00:02:55,615 --> 00:02:58,985
Because of the famine, they travel south, and

59
00:02:59,092 --> 00:03:01,545
Avram asks Sarai to say she is his

60
00:03:01,614 --> 00:03:04,345
sister, fearing the Egyptians will kill him to

61
00:03:04,409 --> 00:03:04,985
take her.

62
00:03:05,358 --> 00:03:09,385
Pharaoh does indeed take Sarai into his palace,

63
00:03:09,405 --> 00:03:12,027
but the Torah tells us, "And the Lord

64
00:03:12,117 --> 00:03:16,107
plagued Pharaoh." Rashi zeroes in on the nature

65
00:03:16,134 --> 00:03:17,147
of these plagues.

66
00:03:17,307 --> 00:03:19,707
He explains that Pharaoh was struck with a

67
00:03:19,777 --> 00:03:22,987
disease called raatan — a skin affliction that

68
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,027
makes marital relations painful and harmful.

69
00:03:26,203 --> 00:03:29,787
This is a remarkably specific detail, and it

70
00:03:29,847 --> 00:03:32,707
matters because Rashi is telling us that God's

71
00:03:32,735 --> 00:03:34,827
intervention was precisely targeted.

72
00:03:35,027 --> 00:03:38,747
The plague wasn't random punishment; it was designed

73
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:41,387
to protect Sarai in the most direct way

74
00:03:41,449 --> 00:03:44,827
possible, by making it physically impossible for Pharaoh

75
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:46,107
to be intimate with her.

76
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:50,987
Rashi reads God's justice as surgical and purposeful,

77
00:03:51,031 --> 00:03:54,744
matching the threat exactly.Now let's jump ahead to

78
00:03:54,764 --> 00:03:56,664
one of the most mysterious scenes in the

79
00:03:56,764 --> 00:04:00,424
portion — the Brit Bein HaBetarim, the Covenant

80
00:04:00,474 --> 00:04:02,984
Between the Parts, in chapter fifteen.

81
00:04:03,204 --> 00:04:05,784
God tells Avram that his descendants will be

82
00:04:05,848 --> 00:04:08,904
strangers in a land not their own, enslaved

83
00:04:08,944 --> 00:04:11,304
and oppressed for four hundred years.

84
00:04:11,464 --> 00:04:13,784
But when you count the actual years of

85
00:04:13,844 --> 00:04:16,494
slavery in Egypt as described later in the

86
00:04:16,522 --> 00:04:19,551
Torah, it doesn't add up to four hundred.

87
00:04:19,764 --> 00:04:23,631
Rashi addresses this apparent contradiction by explaining that

88
00:04:23,651 --> 00:04:26,031
the four hundred years are counted not from

89
00:04:26,071 --> 00:04:28,671
the beginning of the Egyptian slavery but from

90
00:04:28,691 --> 00:04:32,111
the birth of Yitzchak, Avraham's son, because from

91
00:04:32,175 --> 00:04:34,911
that moment the descendants of Avraham were living

92
00:04:34,964 --> 00:04:38,351
in a land that was "not theirs." Rashi

93
00:04:38,404 --> 00:04:41,711
is doing something characteristic here — he's harmonizing

94
00:04:41,791 --> 00:04:43,632
two parts of the Torah that seem to

95
00:04:43,694 --> 00:04:46,912
conflict, showing that the text is precise if

96
00:04:46,952 --> 00:04:50,657
you know how to read it carefully.Another beautiful

97
00:04:50,764 --> 00:04:53,937
Rashi comment comes when God changes Avram's name

98
00:04:53,990 --> 00:04:56,177
to Avraham in chapter seventeen.

99
00:04:56,377 --> 00:04:59,457
The Torah says God told him, "Your name

100
00:04:59,497 --> 00:05:02,097
shall no longer be called Avram, but your

101
00:05:02,177 --> 00:05:04,977
name shall be Avraham, for I have made

102
00:05:05,017 --> 00:05:07,537
you the father of a multitude of nations."

103
00:05:07,777 --> 00:05:12,577
Rashi explains the linguistics: Avram means "father of

104
00:05:12,657 --> 00:05:15,697
Aram," referring to his place of origin, but

105
00:05:15,777 --> 00:05:20,497
Avraham means "father of a multitude," av hamon.

106
00:05:20,577 --> 00:05:22,977
The letter hei is added to his name.

107
00:05:23,244 --> 00:05:25,777
Rashi notes that the letter reish in the

108
00:05:25,830 --> 00:05:29,297
original name, which pointed to Aram, is retained,

109
00:05:29,327 --> 00:05:32,257
because Avraham doesn't lose his past — he

110
00:05:32,326 --> 00:05:33,297
transcends it.

111
00:05:33,457 --> 00:05:36,817
His identity expands from being a local figure

112
00:05:36,897 --> 00:05:39,617
to a universal one, the father of many

113
00:05:39,697 --> 00:05:42,337
nations, and even the letters of his name

114
00:05:42,417 --> 00:05:47,128
carry that transformation.Finally, let's look at the circumcision

115
00:05:47,219 --> 00:05:49,288
itself at the end of the portion.

116
00:05:49,448 --> 00:05:53,371
The Torah describes both Avraham's circumcision and that

117
00:05:53,424 --> 00:05:56,011
of his son Yishmael, who was thirteen years

118
00:05:56,111 --> 00:05:56,651
old.

119
00:05:56,864 --> 00:06:00,269
In describing Yishmael's circumcision, the Torah uses the

120
00:06:00,402 --> 00:06:04,189
Hebrew word "et" — a grammatical particle that

121
00:06:04,245 --> 00:06:07,069
indicates a direct object — in the phrase

122
00:06:07,229 --> 00:06:10,669
"the flesh of his foreskin." But when describing

123
00:06:10,749 --> 00:06:14,749
Avraham's circumcision, this word "et" is absent.

124
00:06:14,962 --> 00:06:18,909
Rashi notices this tiny grammatical difference and offers

125
00:06:18,949 --> 00:06:20,749
a striking explanation.

126
00:06:20,989 --> 00:06:23,709
He says that Avraham, as an elderly man

127
00:06:23,769 --> 00:06:25,789
who had lived a full life, had a

128
00:06:25,856 --> 00:06:29,709
foreskin that was already naturally flattened, so circumcision

129
00:06:29,771 --> 00:06:31,949
required only the cutting of the skin.

130
00:06:32,129 --> 00:06:35,309
But Yishmael, being a young boy, required a

131
00:06:35,357 --> 00:06:38,509
more complete procedure — both cutting and the

132
00:06:38,559 --> 00:06:40,429
further step of uncovering.

133
00:06:40,569 --> 00:06:44,909
The extra word "et" signals this additional element.

134
00:06:45,096 --> 00:06:48,349
It's a perfect example of Rashi's method: a

135
00:06:48,406 --> 00:06:52,029
single small word, easily overlooked, becomes a window

136
00:06:52,093 --> 00:06:54,749
into the physical reality of the story and

137
00:06:54,769 --> 00:06:59,790
the precision of the Torah's language.Stepping back, what

138
00:06:59,870 --> 00:07:03,310
emerges from Rashi's commentary on Lech-Lecha is a

139
00:07:03,358 --> 00:07:05,790
deep conviction that every word in the Torah

140
00:07:05,843 --> 00:07:08,510
is chosen with care, and that no detail

141
00:07:08,563 --> 00:07:09,550
is accidental.

142
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:12,510
Whether it's the order of three phrases in

143
00:07:12,590 --> 00:07:15,710
God's opening command, the nature of a plague

144
00:07:15,763 --> 00:07:18,510
in Egypt, or the presence or absence of

145
00:07:18,550 --> 00:07:22,269
a tiny grammatical particle, Rashi shows us that

146
00:07:22,270 --> 00:07:25,242
the Torah rewards close, patient reading.

147
00:07:25,413 --> 00:07:29,242
Across this portion, we also see Rashi painting

148
00:07:29,402 --> 00:07:31,482
a portrait of a God who is precise

149
00:07:31,535 --> 00:07:35,162
in justice, generous in promise, and deeply attentive

150
00:07:35,242 --> 00:07:39,642
to human experience — emotional, physical, and spiritual.

151
00:07:39,842 --> 00:07:42,362
And we see Avraham not as a flat

152
00:07:42,442 --> 00:07:45,642
heroic figure, but as a real person navigating

153
00:07:45,730 --> 00:07:49,642
sacrifice, fear, faith, and transformation.

154
00:07:50,242 --> 00:07:53,084
Rashi invites us to read the Torah not

155
00:07:53,164 --> 00:07:55,804
faster, but slower — and to trust that

156
00:07:55,844 --> 00:07:57,964
the deeper we look, the more we will

157
00:07:58,076 --> 00:08:02,278
find.Thank you so much for joining me on

158
00:08:02,358 --> 00:08:04,278
Rashi in Brief this week.

159
00:08:04,465 --> 00:08:08,038
Shabbat shalom, and I'll see you next time.

